{{also|hƿæt}}
==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|hƿæt}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*hwat}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*kʷod}}. Cognates include {{cog|ofs|hwet}}, {{cog|osx|hwat}}, {{cog|odt|wat}}, {{cog|goh|hwaz}}, {{cog|non|hvat}}, {{cog|got|𐍈𐌰}}. The Indo-European root also led to {{cog|la|quod}}, {{cog|lt|kàd}}, and {{cog|ga|cad}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ʍæt/|lang=ang}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|ang|pronoun|g=n}}

# [[what]]
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=trkLAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA188 The Dialogues of Solomon and Saturn]''
#*: '''''Hwæt''' sind þā þrēo þing þe nān mann būtan libban ne mæġ?''
#*:: '''What''' are the three things no one can live without?
#* '''c. 995''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://archive.org/stream/grammatik00aelfuoft#page/n129/mode/2up ''Extracts on Grammar in English'']
#*: ''Dǣdlīcu word ġeswitolaþ '''hwæt''' menn dōþ.''
#*:: Active verbs express '''what''' people do.
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=2xobVW92JLcC&pg=PA182 "Midlent Sunday"]
#*: ''Mid '''hwām''' mage wē byċġan hlāf þissum folce?''
#*:: '''What''' can we use to buy bread for these people?
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=nnA7AQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA54 Lives of Saints]''
#*: '''''Hwæs''' ābītst þū?''
#*:: '''What''' are you waiting for?
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=8UQrAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA116 The Legend of St. Andrew]''
#*: ''Ealle menn flēoþ of þǣre ċeastre. Tō '''hwon''' wille ġē þider faran?''
#*:: Everyone's fleeing that city. '''What''' do you want to go there for?
# [[why]]
#* '''c. 900''', ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=At1SV29bH4oC&pg=PA38 The Consolation of Philosophy]''
#*: '''''Hwæt''' seofast þū wiþ mē?''
#*:: '''Why''' are you groaning at me?
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n197/mode/2up John 4:27]
#*: ''His leorningcneohtas wundrodon þæt hē wiþ wīf spræc, þēah heora nān ne cwæþ "Hwæt sēcst þū?" oþþe "'''Hwæt''' spricst þū wiþ hīe?"
#*:: His disciples were amazed that he was talking to a woman, though none of them said "What are you looking for?" or "'''Why''' are you talking to her?"
# [[how many]] or [[how much]] ''(+genitive)''
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=Cd4UAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA242 "Palm Sunday: On the Lord's Passion"]
#*: ''Iūdās sōna ēode tō þāra Iudēisċena rǣde and openlīċe befrān '''hwæt''' hīe him fēos ġeūðen ġif hē þone Hǣlend him belǣwan mihte.''
#*:: Judas immediately went to the Jewish council and openly asked '''how much''' money they would give him for betraying Jesus.
#* '''c. 900''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=Bs1nAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA57 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius']] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: ''Be þām hringum man meahte witan '''hwæt''' Rōmāna duguþe ġefeallen wæs, for þon þe hit wæs þēaw mid him on þām dagum þæt nān ōðer ne mōste gyldenne hring werian būtan hē æðeles cynnes wǣre.''
#*:: You could tell by the rings '''how much''' of the Roman nobility had fallen, because the custom back then was that no one could wear a gold ring unless they were from a noble family.
#* '''c. 900''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=LKe0AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA220 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius']] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: ''Hwæðer Rōmāne hit witon nū ǣnigum menn tō seċġenne '''hwæt''' heora folces on Ispāniam on fēawum ġēarum forwurde?''
#*:: Did the Romans know any longer '''how many''' of their people had perished in Spain within a few years?
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=trkLAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA204 The Dialogues of Solomon and Saturn]''
#*: ''Saga mē '''hwæt''' fisċcynna sīe.''
#*:: Tell me '''how many''' species of fish there are.
# [[anything]], [[something]]
#* '''c. 900''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=xBNXAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA71 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius']] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: ''Sēo lēo bringþ hungrigum hwelpum '''hwæt''' tō etenne.''
#*:: The lion brings hungry cubs '''something''' to eat.
#* '''c. 1011''', {{w|Byrhtferth}}, ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=7J9JAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA335 Manual]''
#*: ''Ġif þū tōdǣlst '''hwæt''' on fēower, se [[feorþa dæl|fēorða dǣl]] biþ quadrans ġeċīeġed, and þā ōðre dōdrantēs bēoþ ġenemnede.''
#*:: If you divide '''anything''' into fours, one part is called a ''quadrans'' (Latin for 'one fourth'), and the other three are called ''dodrantes'' (Latin for 'three fourths').
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=eDdECgAAQBAJ&pg=PA161 "Preface to Genesis"]
#*: ''Þā unġelǣredan prēostas, ġif hīe '''hwæt''' lȳtles of þām Lǣdenbōcum understandaþ, þonne þyncþ him sōna þæt hīe mæġen mǣre lārēowas bēon.''
#*:: If uneducated priests understand '''anything''' at all (lit. 'anything little') from the Latin texts, they immediately think they can be famous teachers.

